  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Given that $det(A)=3$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $-4\\text{det}({A}^{T})+2\\text{det}(I)=3$\n- $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=6$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=1$",
  "worked_solution": "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-4\\text{det}({A}^{T})+2\\text{det}(I)=-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1=-10\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=3-1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
